      "section": "results",
      "text": "tion behavior of short-chain PFAA, which will likely present the greatest challenge for barrier design. Particularly, the effect of ionic strength and DOM with different MW distributions is less often studied for short- chain PFAA and will likely impact barrier performance. Second, only one CAC (PlumeStop \u00ae ) has been evaluated for PFAS adsorption [26] and due to differences in carbon surface chemistries, other CAC mate - rials should be considered. To address these gaps, the objectives of this study are to (1) measure the impact of ionic strength and composition, DOM with different MW compositions, and the addition of stabilizing polymer on the adsorption of PFAA to CAC as a function of chain length, (2) correlate batch adsorption results with CAC surface chemistry and PFAA structure to provide insight into adsorption mechanisms, and (3) quantify the relative magnitude of each impact on predicted in situ barrier longevity. To our knowledge, this work is the first to examine the impact of DOM with varying MW on the adsorption of short-chain PFAA and the first to report in situ CAC barrier longevity modeling results for short-chain PFAA under perturbations in water chemistry. The com - mercial CAC evaluated here is also currently being used at field sites in Germany and in other EU countries. By focusing on the most relevant"
