      "text": "Subsurface injection of colloidal activated carbon (CAC) is an emerging in situ approach to treat perfluoroalkyl acid (PFAA)-impacted groundwater. However, long-term effectiveness is uncertain for short- chain PFAA and for a range of water chemistry scenarios. The impact of inorganic ions, dissolved organic matter (DOM), and CAC delivery polymer on the adsorption of long- and short-chain PFAA were measured experimentally. Our results and modeling indicates that these water quality variables significantly lower retention and expected per - formance of short-chain PFAA in CAC barriers. Quantitative data will support more accurate longevity assessments and future barrier design. CRediT authorship contribution statement Rachel Mol \u00b4e : Writing \u2013 review & editing, Writing \u2013 original draft, Methodology, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation, Conceptu - alization. Adriana Velosa: Writing \u2013 review & editing, Writing \u2013 orig - inal draft, Supervision. Grant Carey: Writing \u2013 review & editing, Writing \u2013 original draft, Methodology, Formal analysis, Conceptualiza - tion. Xitong Liu: Writing \u2013 review & editing, Writing \u2013 original draft, Supervision, Project administration, Funding acquisition, Conceptuali - zation. Guangbin Li: Writing \u2013 review & editing, Writing \u2013 original draft, Supervision, Conceptualization. Dimin Fan: Writing \u2013 review &"
