      "section": "introduction",
      "text": "due to minimal CAC surface coverage. Longevity modeling demonstrated that groundwater solutes limit the capacity for PFAA in a CAC barrier, particularly for short-chain PFAA. 1. Introduction Per- and polyfluorinated alkyl substances (PFAS) are a class of organofluorine compounds that are environmentally persistent and are detected at low ( \u00b5 g /L to ng/L) concentrations in aquatic matrices [1-6] . PFAS are found in many consumer products and in aqueous film-forming foams (AFFF) used for firefighting which significantly impacts ground - water at airports and US military sites [7,8] . PFAS have been linked to reproductive issues, thyroid disease, and immunotoxicity [2,9,10] . Perfluorooctanoic acid (PFOA) and perfluorooctane sulfonate (PFOS) are the most frequently studied perfluoroalkyl acids (PFAA) due to their widespread historical use, but were voluntarily phased out in the mid-2000 \u2032 s. Short-chain PFAA ( < 7 perfluorinated carbons), such as perfluorobutanoic acid (PFBA) and perfluorobutane sulfonate (PFBS), have been introduced as alternatives and are more water soluble and mobile in porous media [11-13] . In April of 2024, the US Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) announced its National Primary Drinking Water Regulation (NPDWR) that set enforceable Maximum Contaminant"
